<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>1.3. The operator ``del''</title>
  <p>
  <alert>MTH 401</alert>
  </p>

<definition xml:id="def-divergence">
  <statement>
    <p>Let <m>\mbf F(x,y,z) = (F_1, F_2, F_3)</m>. 
      The
  <term>divergence</term> of <m>\mbf F</m> is given by
  <md> \displaystyle
  \nabla\cdot\mbf F  =
  \left(
    \frac{\partial}{\partial x},
    \frac{\partial}{\partial y},
    \frac{\partial}{\partial z}
  \right)\cdot(F_1, F_2, F_3)  =
  \frac{\partial F_1}{\partial x} +
  \frac{\partial F_2}{\partial y} +
  \frac{\partial F_3}{\partial z}. 
  </md>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>


<definition xml:id="def-curl">
  <statement>
    <p>Let <m>\mbf F(x,y,z) = (F_1, F_2, F_3)</m>. 
      The <term>curl</term> of <m>\mbf F</m> is
      <md> \displaystyle
      \nabla\times\mbf F =
      \left|
        \begin{array}{ccc}
          \vec{i} \amp  \vec{j} \amp  \vec{k} \\
          \frac{\partial}{\partial x}
                  \amp 
                    \frac{\partial}{\partial y}
                            \amp 
                              \frac{\partial}{\partial z}      \\
          F_1 \amp  F_2 \amp  F_3 \\
        \end{array}
      \right| =
      \left(
        \pd{F_3}{y}-\pd{F_2}{z}, 
        \pd{F_1}{z}-\pd{F_3}{x},
        \pd{F_2}{x}-\pd{F_1}{y}
      \right).
      </md>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>\displaystyle \mbf F = (xy+yz, 3x+2yz, 2xy + z^2).</m>  Find the
         divergence <m>\nabla\cdot \mbf F</m>, and notice that the divergence is a
         function, rather than a field.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="show-soln">
      <p>
        <m>\ds \nabla\cdot \mbf F = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(xy+yz)+\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(3x+2yz)+\frac{\partial}{\partial z}(2xy+z^2)=y+2z+2z=y+4z</m>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
  Find <m>\nabla\times\mbf F</m> where <m>\mbf F = (xy+yz, 3x+2yz, 2xy +
  z^2)</m>, and notice that the result in this case is a new field.</p>
    </statement>
  <solution component="show-soln">
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\nabla\times\mbf F \amp = \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x}(2xy+z^2)-\frac{\partial}{\partial z}(3x+2yz), \frac{\partial}{\partial z}(xy+yz)-\frac{\partial}{\partial x}(2xy+z^2), \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(3x+2yz)-\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(xy+yz) \right) </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = (2x-2y, y-2y, 3-x-z) </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = (2x-2y, -y, 3-x-z) </mrow>
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution></exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>The divergence <m>\nabla\cdot\mbf F</m> can be defined for fields in
  any dimension, but the curl <m>\nabla\times\mbf F</m> only works in
  dimension three.  However, if we have a two-dimensional field <m>\mbf
  F(x,y)=(F_1(x,y), F_2(x,y))</m>, we can view it as a three-dimensional
  field by setting the third component to 0:  <m>\mbf F = (F_1, F_2,
  0)</m>.  Then we have
  <md> \displaystyle
  \nabla \times \mbf F = \left(0,0,\pd{F_2}{x}-\pd{F_1}{y}\right),
  </md>
  which we often just think of as the function
  <md>\nabla \times \mbf F = \pd{F_2}{x}-\pd{F_1}{y}.</md></p>
      <p>Find <m>\nabla\times\mbf F</m> if <m>\mbf F(x,y) = (-y, x)</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  <solution component="show-soln">
      <p>
        <m>\nabla\times \mbf F = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x)-\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(-y) = 1+1 = 2</m> (in direction <m>\vec{k}</m>)
      </p>
    </solution></exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>Just as we found second derivatives in Calculus I, we can
  differentiate twice using <m>\nabla</m> provided that we interpret
  <m>\nabla</m> appropriately.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Start with the function <m>f(x,y,z)</m> and write the gradient <m>\nabla
  f</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="show-soln">
        <p>
          <m>\ds \nabla f = \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}, \frac{\partial f}{\partial z} \right)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Then find the curl of the gradient:  <m>\nabla\times\nabla f</m>.  (It's
  kind of a gross computation, but just be patient!)</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="show-soln">
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>\nabla\times\nabla f \amp = \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}) - \frac{\partial}{\partial z}(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}), 
              \frac{\partial}{\partial z}(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}) - \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}), 
              \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}) - \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}) \right) </mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \left(\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y\partial z}-\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial z\partial y}, 
                \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial z\partial x}-\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x\partial z} , 
                \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x\partial y} -\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y\partial x} \right) </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = (0,0,0) </mrow>
          </md>
          since mixed partials are equal!  Well that worked out...
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What does this say about the curl <m>\nabla\times\mbf F</m> of a
  conservative field?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="show-soln">
        <p>
          If <m>\mbf F</m> is a conservative field, then <m>\mbf F = \nabla f</m>, so 
          <m>\nabla \times \mbf F = \nabla \times (\nabla f) = \vec{0}</m>. (That is, 
          conservative fields have curl zero.)
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
 </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>Now suppose that <m>\mbf F=(F_1, F_2, F_3)</m> and find the
  divergence of the curl: <m>\nabla\cdot(\nabla\times\mbf F)</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="show-soln">
      <p>Note again, mixed partial derivatives don't matter which order we differentiate...
        <md>
          <mrow>\nabla \cdot (\nabla\times \mbf F) \amp = 
            \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial}{\partial y}, \frac{\partial}{\partial z}  \right) \cdot 
          \left( \frac{\partial F_3}{\partial y}-\frac{\partial F_2}{\partial z}, 
          \frac{\partial F_1}{\partial z}-\frac{\partial F_3}{\partial x}, 
          \frac{\partial F_2}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial F_1}{\partial y}  \right) </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp =  \frac{\partial^2 F_3}{\partial x \partial y} -\frac{\partial^2 F_2}{\partial x \partial y}
            +\frac{\partial^2 F_1}{\partial y \partial z} - \frac{\partial^2 F_3}{\partial y \partial x}
            +\frac{\partial^2 F_2}{\partial z \partial x} - \frac{\partial^2 F_1}{\partial z \partial y}
          </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = 0 </mrow>
        </md>
        So for any field, the divergence of the curl of a field is 0.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2.5in">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        There is a very important operation we will use often, called the <em>Laplacian</em>. Let's explore.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>f(x,y,z)</m> is a function, find an expression for the
  divergence of the gradient: <m>\nabla\cdot\nabla f</m>.</p>
        <p><em>Note:</em> We call this the <em>Laplacian</em> of <m>f</m> and denote it as
  <m>\nabla^2f = \nabla\cdot\nabla f</m>.  It will play an important role
  later in the course.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="show-soln">
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow> \nabla \cdot \nabla f \amp = 
              \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial}{\partial y}, \frac{\partial}{\partial z}  \right) \cdot \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}, \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}  \right) </mrow>
              <mrow> \amp =  \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2} +  \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y^2} +  \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial z^2}</mrow>
          </md>
          
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find <m>\displaystyle \nabla^2 \ln(x^2+y^2)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="show-soln">
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow> \nabla^2 \ln(x^2+y^2) \amp =  \frac{\partial^2}{\partial x^2}\left(\ln(x^2+y^2)\right) +\frac{\partial^2}{\partial y^2}\left(\ln(x^2+y^2)\right)  </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\frac{2x}{x^2+y^2} \right) +\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{2y}{x^2+y^2} \right)  </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = \frac{(x^2+y^2)(2) - 2x(2x)}{(x^2+y^2)^2} + \frac{(x^2+y^2)(2) - 2y(2y)}{(x^2+y^2)^2} </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = \frac{2x^2 + 2y^2 - 4x^2+2x^2+2y^2-4y^2}{(x^2+y^2)^2}  </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = 0 </mrow>
          </md>
          
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that <m>\ds F(x,y) =
  \left(
    -\frac{y}{x^2+y^2},
    \frac{x}{x^2+y^2}
  \right)</m>.
  Find <m>\nabla\times\mbf F</m> and <m>\nabla \cdot \mbf F</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="show-soln">
      <p>
        First the curl.
        <md>
          <mrow>\nabla\times\mbf F \amp = \left(0,0,  \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\frac{x}{x^2+y^2} \right) -\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{-y}{x^2+y^2} \right)  \right)</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp =  \left(0,0,  \frac{x^2+y^2-2x^2}{(x^2+y^2)^2}  -\left(\frac{(x^2+y^2)(-1)+y(2y)}{(x^2+y^2)^2} \right)  \right)</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = \left(0,0, \frac{-x^2+y^2-(-x^2+y^2)}{(x^2+y^2)^2}   \right) </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = 0 </mrow>
        </md>
        Now the divergence.
        <md>
          <mrow>\nabla\cdot\mbf F \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\frac{-y}{x^2+y^2} \right) +\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{x}{x^2+y^2} \right) </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp =  \frac{(x^2+y^2)(0)-y(2x)}{(x^2+y^2)^2}  + \frac{0-x(2y)}{(x^2+y^2)^2}</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp =  \frac{2xy-2xy}{(x^2+y^2)^2}  </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = 0 </mrow>
        </md>
        
        
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
